Time touches by the platform's event time.

What: `TouchEvent::time_stamp: Option<Instant>` (Flutter's `PointerEvent.timeStamp`): when the platform says the
touch happened, on the executor's clock (`BackgroundExecutor::now`; `scheduler::Instant`, which is
`performance.now()` on the web and the OS monotonic clock elsewhere). Each backend moves its OS event time onto that
clock by the event's age (now minus how long ago the OS says the touch was): gpui_ios from `UITouch.timestamp`
against `NSProcessInfo.systemUptime`, gpui_web from `Event.timeStamp` against `performance.now()` (a
`wasm_bindgen` extern, as `http_client.rs` binds `fetch`: no manifest change). The touch recognizer's velocity
tracker and the pinch arena's scale recognizer sample at the event's time stamp, as Flutter's trackers take
`timeStamp`; timers, deadlines and momentum ticks stay on the dispatch clock, as Flutter's run on its own timers. A `None` time stamp (a platform without event times, synthesized touches, tests)
is timed at dispatch, explicitly (`event.time_stamp.unwrap_or(now)`, once per recognizer). Adds a recognizer test:
the same 16 ms samples dispatched on time, at 33 ms frames and 50 ms late fling at the same velocity. Every
`TouchEvent` literal gains the field.

Why: GPUI timed a touch when it dispatched it. A browser delivers touch moves at its frame rate, late and in
bunches (headless Chromium every ~33 ms), and Android batches moves per input frame, so the gaps the velocity
tracker saw were not the finger's: least squares read the web's jittered samples as a slowing finger and flung
~40 % shorter than the same swipe on iOS (Gate 2 trace replay). Flutter times every sample by the platform's event
time for this reason. `TouchEvent` is GPUI's type and the recognizers are private, so neither a Parallax crate nor
an extension trait can add it. No cfg: the field is core; each backend fills it from its own clock.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

